  "leakage_audit": {
    "passed": true,
    "checks": [
      {
        "id": 1,
        "check": "No overlapping districts between TRAIN and TEST",
        "passed": true,
        "detail": "Checked all 5 seeds; train\u2229test was empty in every one."
      },
      {
        "id": 2,
        "check": "No overlapping districts between VALIDATION and TEST",
        "passed": true,
        "detail": "Checked all 5 seeds; val\u2229test was empty in every one. (train\u2229val was also empty in every seed.)"
      },
      {
        "id": 3,
        "check": "Scalers fit ONLY on training data",
        "passed": true,
        "detail": "StandardScaler.fitted_on='train', fitted on 325 rows, which equals the training split's row count (325). build_split_datasets() computes the scaler from the training frame and then applies that same fitted object to val and test."
      },
      {
        "id": 4,
        "check": "Imputers fit ONLY on training data",
        "passed": true,
        "detail": "No imputation is performed anywhere in this pipeline, because none is needed: the 561 fully-aligned examples contain 0 missing values across all 12 features. Rows lacking any modality were excluded up front (counted in the exclusion report) rather than imputed. Vacuously satisfied."
      },
      {
        "id": 5,
        "check": "Baseline mean computed ONLY from training labels",
        "passed": true,
        "detail": "TrainMeanBaseline.fit() received 325 labels (= training rows) and produced 3.0964 t/ha, exactly the training-label mean. The test split's own mean is 3.0388 t/ha and is never read during fitting - the two differ, confirming the baseline is not secretly the test mean."
      },
      {
        "id": 6,
        "check": "Test data never used for early stopping / checkpoint selection",
        "passed": true,
        "detail": "train_model()'s signature is (train_ds, val_ds, seed, epochs, batch_size, lr, weight_decay, patience, hidden_dims, dropout) - it accepts no test dataset, so it is structurally incapable of reading one. An AST walk of its executable body (docstring excluded) finds 0 referenced name(s) containing 'test' (none), and confirms early stopping keys on `val_loss`/`best_val`. The test split is touched exactly once per (seed, configuration), by the caller, after training returns."
      },
      {
        "id": 7,
        "check": "Target not included as a feature",
        "passed": true,
        "detail": "Target column is 'final_yield_t_ha'. The 12 feature columns across all configurations are ['satellite_evi_mean', 'satellite_ndvi_mean', 'satellite_ndwi_mean', 'soil_clay', 'soil_nitrogen', 'soil_phh2o', 'soil_sand', 'soil_soc', 'weather_humidity_pct_mean', 'weather_precip_mm_sum', 'weather_temp_c_mean', 'weather_wind_speed_ms_mean'] - the target is not among them. training/district_dataset.assert_no_forbidden_features() raises at dataset construction if it ever were."
      },
      {
        "id": 8,
        "check": "Source URLs / provenance strings not used as features",
        "passed": true,
        "detail": "No feature column contains a source or URL (none found). yield_source_url, weather_source, satellite_source and soil_source are all in FORBIDDEN_AS_FEATURES. This matters concretely here: Tamil Nadu's rows cite different source URLs than Andhra Pradesh's, so a source string is a perfect region fingerprint."
      },
      {
        "id": 9,
        "check": "District / state identifiers not used as features",
        "passed": true,
        "detail": "No feature column is an identifier (none found). state, district, canonical_district_name and district_id are all in FORBIDDEN_AS_FEATURES and are kept as metadata only. Additionally `year` and `season` are excluded despite being ordinary columns: in this dataset every Tamil Nadu example is 'Whole Year' in 2019/2024 while every AP/Telangana example is Kharif/Rabi in 1999-2012, so either column would identify the region perfectly and defeat the unseen-district design."
      }
    ],
